- F-34 **Under the switch-over's setting the code generator refuses 38 declarations of the fourteen components that the distance stage checks, in 10 kinds, all of them declarations that the checker passes.** The setting is the distance stage's (F-53): the checker's failures caught per declaration, and the bound `Any`. Measured once, on `dbf4825e0`'s code, by `compile-ladder/plan-25/probes/codegen.sh` with its 288 helper strings put back on their glue classes; before local functions compiled, the same probe gave 47 in 11 kinds. The kinds that F-315 removes (`Any` in an `extends` clause, `forbid` and the `test` modifier) and the local function (F-320) no longer occur. A declaration counted at its first wall, and no emitted class was run. Each kind left has a ledger row or a plan line, among them varargs (row 624), `asif` (row 818) and "Not in the trait table" (row 733), so after the switch-over the code generator still stands between the library and bytecode. On climb batch 21b's base (`aa5c67d48`) the dispatch generator refused none of `FortressLibrary`'s 178 overload sets. No test checks the 38, since the gate's distance stage runs no code generation. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md`, "5. Programs run old against new; the measure"; `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-walls/REPORT.md`, "5. Programs run old against new, and the probes"; `reviews/phase3-exit/zero-audit.md`, "Part C. The debt carried into phase 4", item 4; `perf-probes/codegen-distance/REPORT.md`, sections 2 to 6.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md, "5. Programs run old against new; the measure"; from: reviews/batch-26-review.md, "Findings"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: the pass on the gather's rewrite: the probe's quoted output lines, and the nine declarations that F-320 holds, left to the report; the tree named by its commit, not by climb batch 26's rung L; "No test checks it" said of the 38. The review's correction of the ground: the 38 in 10 kinds were measured on rung L's tree, dbf4825e0; the merged tree adds rung T's three overloads over String to FortressLibrary, plain Fortress code that the code generator has not measured.

- F-299 **Walk loads a `builtinPrimitive` string that names a static helper of `nativeHelpers/` beside its glue classes, and 306 of the one library's 449 live bindings name one.** It builds P-58, "Every native binding is a ...", whose compiled half F-298 gives. The loader (`interpreter/glue/StaticNativeApp.java`) calls the overload whose Java types carry the declared types, `self` first, and raises a helper's `fortress.<Api>$<Name>` as the library's `<Name>`. It refuses at load a string that names no helper or fits none, and a declared type that no Java type carries, `self`'s among them, read from the declaring type. The library's own `FlatString`, identified by the component that declares it, carries `self` in a Java `String`; a program's own type named `FlatString` or `JavaString` carries none. A helper parameter declared `String` is refused at load, 'no Java type carries the declared type of parameter', since a Java `String` cannot carry a string that is not flat; one declared `FlatString` or `JavaString`, and a result declared `String`, are carried in a Java `String`. Its departures: a generic owner's dotted method is checked only where an instance is made (row 864); the declaring type is read, not the value's, so a program's object that extends a carried trait against its `comprises` clause passes the helper a value that no Java type carries (row 551); a glue class bound over `String` receives a non-flat string's type name, and so would a helper bound in a method of the trait `String`, as `self`; the library binds no helper there (row 846). The 306 by type: `ZZ32` 25 and `Int` 3, `ZZ64` 26 and `ZZ` 22 (F-306; their `CHOOSE` F-305, `REM` and `MOD` F-312, divisors F-314), `Float` 57 and the top-level `fromRawBits` (F-303), `RR32` 50 (F-310, its `asFloat` F-311), the `asFloat`s of `Long` and `BigNum` 2 (F-311), the small rest 8 (F-307), and `NN32` 27, `NN64` 24, `UnsignedLong` 3 and `Char` 39 (F-308, F-313), the top-level `nanoTime` (F-314), and the string family's 18: 11 of `FlatString`'s 13 methods, all but `=` and the `flatConcat` overload over a `Char`; `natLineSeparator`; `match`; and `printThreadInfo`, `printWithThread`, `printlnWithThread`, `getEnvironment` and `getProperty`, each a native over `FlatString` beside an overload over `String` that flattens in a `typecase`; the other 143 name glue classes. The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/interpreter.md`, "Natives", gives the carriers and the messages. Test: `tests/NativeHelperBindingWalk.fss`, `NativeHelperRaiseWalk`, `ZZ32DivisionByZeroWalk`, `NativeHelperFamilyZZ32Walk`, `NativeHelperCarriedSelfFlatStringWalk`, `NativeHelperStringRopeWalk`, `compiler_tests/NativeHelperFamilyStringCompiled`, and the refusals `NativeHelperMisspeltWalk`, `NativeHelperMisfitWalk`, `NativeHelperUncarriedWalk`, `NativeHelperUncarriedSelf*Walk` and `NativeHelperNonFlatStringWalk`; the entries of the other families name their own tests. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-natives-walk/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-natives-self/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why"; `compile-ladder/rung-string-natives/REPORT.md`, "What changed and why".
  from: reviews/batch-26-review.md, "Where the text, the brief or the principles split"; from: compile-ladder/rung-string-natives/REPORT.md, "What changed and why"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: the review's finding C (its split 6): the departures name the latent self half of row 846, read in the code at 61f1c6a93: StaticNativeApp.java:103-112, javaForSelf("String") answers javaFor("String"), a Java String, and :282 unboxes it by v.getString(), which a rope answers with its type's name. The pass, for the entry past 600 characters: the loader's method names (javaForSelf(FType), declaringComponent), the families' pointers given what each holds, "told by" and "at a Char" said in words that read one way, the review-queue item of FlatString's = and flatConcat at a Char, and the tests that F-303, F-310, F-311, F-312 and F-314 name cut; "rope" said in plain words.

- F-320 **The code generator compiles a block's local functions as closures, the group recursive and mutually recursive without a mutable cell.** Each local function of a block is a closure class made as a function expression's, bound under its name in the block's environment, so a call of it and its use as a value go through the closure; it reaches itself as its own `this` and each sibling through a field of its class that the block sets once, after the whole group is made and before the body runs (`compiler/codegen/CodeGen.java`). It refuses three forms that the specification allows, each after the checker passes the program, with its own message: a local function with static parameters of its own, two of one name in a block, and the `atomic` modifier (row 304); a varargs one stops at 'Can't compile VarArgs yet' (row 624). Under the switch-over's setting, measured once on `dbf4825e0`'s code, all nine of the library's declarations that stopped at 'Can't compile LetFn' emit (F-34). Gated by `compiler_tests/LocalFunctionCallCompiled`, `LocalFunctionValueCompiled`, `LocalFunctionRecursiveCompiled`, `LocalFunctionCaptureCompiled` and `LocalFunctionGenericContextCompiled`, their `tests/LocalFunction*Walk` guards, and `XXXLocalFunctionGenericCompiled`, `XXXLocalFunctionOverloadedCompiled` and `XXXLocalFunctionAtomicCompiled`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: the pass on the gather's draft: the code generator's method names (forFnExpr, forLetFn, localFunctionClosure, localFunctionOrNull) and the node's name LetFn left to the report's section 1; the nine declarations bounded to the one measurement on dbf4825e0's code that F-34 gives.

- F-252 **Under walk, where the parameter-by-parameter check refuses two declarations with static parameters of their own, or one beside a generic trait's functional method that declares none of its own, the load check reads the pair again on its declared domains, by the No Duplicates, Return Type and Meet Rules.** A declaration with static parameters of its own is a generic function, a generic object's constructor, or a functional method that declares static parameters in a trait or object that has none. Walk's load check first compares two overloads parameter by parameter, and a pair that it finds excluding loads. It reads two generic functions of one name and one list of static parameters at one shared symbolic instantiation, and two instances of one generic object as two object types that cannot be extended, so they exclude. So `o[\T\](x: P[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\])`, with `trait P[\T\] excludes { Q[\T\] }`, excludes at one `T`, though a type that extends `P[\ZZ32\]` and `Q[\String\]` has both. Walk then reads such an exclusion again over the instances of each, and withdraws it where their instances do not exclude. Instances of one generic type exclude only where their static arguments cannot be equal. Any other exclusion stands where the two still exclude with their static parameters renamed apart. Or, read at the shared instantiation, it stands where every generic type that both extend forces their static arguments to be equal, since no type extends two instances of one generic trait: so `Array` and `ImmutableArray` exclude at every pair of instances through `ReadableArray`. A pair whose exclusion is withdrawn is read on its declared domains as a refused pair is. It reads a refused pair of two such declarations again, each over all its own instances, and loads the pair if it meets the three rules. An own generic beside such a functional method is read as two own generics, the trait's static parameters standing for the method's (F-318). A pair that the parameter check orders is read again only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-274). F-275 holds the Return Type Rule. The parameter check does not find two instances of one generic type at different ground static arguments excluding, such as `Tg[\3\]` and `Tg[\4\]`, so two plain declarations so typed are refused (row 416). Two declarations with static parameters of their own so typed load where the reading of the whole parameter type finds the instances apart, as `h[\I\](a: Array[\RR64,I\])` beside `h[\J\](a: Array[\ZZ32,J\])` loads and runs (F-155, F-258). For the Meet Rule, walk looks for a third declaration that covers the overlap only where, at each position, the two parameter types are equal, or one of them is a static parameter, or both are instances of one generic type whose static arguments match in the same way (row 539). This search reads a bound that names its own parameter from the declarations, so two such bounds meet, and unifies a symbolic size with a size parameter. A pair whose domains exclude when read whole also loads, where neither declaration has a `where` clause of its own (F-258; row 787). A pair with a varargs parameter is not read again and keeps the parameter check's refusal (F-257). At a call, walk compares a functional method of a generic trait or object on the instance that the call makes. F-253 gives the case of a generic dotted method, where one of the two declared domains can lie inside the other. Gated by `tests/DispatchRenamedGenericArmWalk`, `DispatchSwappedGenericArmWalk` and `GenericPairNestedBoundsWalk` (load and dispatch), and the expected failure `XXXOverloadExistentialMeetWalk` (row 539); for the withdrawal, as refusals at load, by `GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk`, `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` and `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`, and as programs that load, by `GenericBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericFBoundsMeetWalk`, `GenericPairExcludingWalk`, `GenericSizePairNestedWalk`, `ShuffleTest` and `GenericPairExcludesTogetherWalk`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-generic-pairs/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 5; `compile-ladder/rung-walk-overload-if/REPORT.md` section 1, "Row 373", and section 9.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-vector-scalar/REPORT.md, "8. Sentences made false"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: the pass on the gather's rewrite, for the entry past 600 characters: the code reference of the size unification (types/SymbolicNat.unifyStaticArg) left to the report; the generic dotted method's case at a call, which F-253 states, given by its pointer; in the title, "that declares none of its own" attached to the method, which the cold reader read two ways.

- F-257 **Under walk, a pair of declarations that the load check refuses after it reads the pair on its declared domains is refused in the name of the rule that the pair breaks.** The load check reads a pair on its declared domains where its parameter-by-parameter check refuses the pair and both declarations have static parameters of their own, or one has them and the other is a plain declaration or a generic trait's functional method that declares none of its own (F-252, F-27, F-318). It reads a pair that the check orders only for the No Duplicates Rule (F-274). The messages open "fails the Return Type Rule:" and "fails the Meet Rule:", and the No Duplicates Rule's is walk's "fails because their parameter lists have the same types"; the report gives them whole. The first two contain the words "not found" because walk's reading is narrower than the rules: rows 539, 550 and 779 hold valid pairs that walk refuses. A pair that is not read on its declared domains keeps the parameter check's message, such as "with generic type, at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types". Such a pair has a varargs parameter, or parameter lists of different lengths, or raises an error while its instances are read. Or it is of none of the kinds above, as two functional methods of generic traits, or it is the pair of instances that an explicit instantiation such as `f[\A, B\]` makes of two generic declarations of one name (`XXXGenericOverload2`). The message does not decide which pairs load. Gated by `tests/GenericReturnRuleInvariantWalk`, `GenericReturnRuleOuterParamWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainReturnRuleWalk` (Return Type), `GenericOverloadInstancesApartWalk`, `GenericBoundsNoMeetWalk` and `GenericBesidePlainNoMeetWalk` (Meet), and `NoDuplicatesRuleWalk` and `NoDuplicatesRuleSizeWalk` (No Duplicates). Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md` sections 1 and 3.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-walk-vector-scalar/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: made false by climb batch 26's rung W (c41c3d2b0), which cites.md lists as naming F-252 and which the gather did not rewrite: walk now reads an own generic beside a functional method of a generic trait without static parameters of its own on the declared domains (OverloadedFunction.java:772-777, validOnDeclaredDomains, read at 61f1c6a93) and refuses it by rule name (tests/GenericInsideTraitMethodReturnRuleWalk); "a functional method of a generic trait beside another" said as two such methods, which validOnDeclaredDomains does not read. The Return Type and Meet messages cut to their opening words, whole in rung-walk-trait-method-bounds/REPORT.md section 1, so that the entry grows only by its clause; "say 'not found'" said as the words that the two messages contain.

- F-181 **The code generator compiles a function, method, object or trait declaration that carries a `where` clause in its own header, emitting nothing for the clause, and refuses every function or method with a contract (row 701).** The clause's constraints are the checker's. A trait's or object's contract does not reach the refusal: the disambiguator drops it (row 781); F-255 says how each path reads a `where` clause on an `extends` entry (row 774). Walk checks a function's `requires` clause and raises `CallerViolation`. The `fortress-repo` skill's `references/compiler.md`, "What the compiled path cannot compile yet", gives the refusal and the constructs that the code generator has no case for; an array comprehension has no case either. A local function compiles as a closure, except in the four forms that F-320 names. A trait whose `comprises` clause lists a type at a `where`-clause size, of `NatParam`'s shape, compiles; a call that opens its value to the size fails verification (row 307). Where a generic type that such a clause lists gives a member's body, the code generator crashes first (row 812). A function's or method's own clause that introduces a `where`-clause variable compiles, and the compiled call dies with `NoClassDefFoundError` (row 867). Tests: `compiler_tests/WhereBoundFunctionCompiled`, `WhereBoundMethodCompiled`, `WhereBoundObjectCompiled`, `WhereBoundTraitMethodCompiled`, `ComprisesWhereFunctionalMethodCompiled`; `XXXFunctionContractCodeGeneration`; the `XXXNatOpenRun` pair; the `XXXWhereVariableCallCompiled` pair. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-codegen-where/REPORT.md`, sections 1, 2 and 5.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-codegen-local-functions/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: the pass on the gather's rewrite: "the three forms of row 304" left out the varargs local function, which F-320 names too (row 624); the exceptions given by F-320, which names all four.

- P-159 **A float compared with a NaN answers: `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` give `false`, and `CMP` gives `Unordered`, on both paths.** On `RR64` and `RR32` the four ordering operators answer `false` when either side is a NaN, and `CMP` answers `Unordered`, as the team built them. `FloatingComparisonError` stays declared in both libraries, and nothing raises it. The specification's overview of operators says that this comparison throws that error, and the sentence before it that a rational comparison at 1/0 or 0/0 throws `RationalComparisonError` (`Specification/basic/operators/opr-overview.tex:283-288`). Both sentences are revised, in the form of Appendix I, to the rationals chapter's reading: 0/0 compares `false` "for compatibility with floating-point arithmetic", and the infinities are ordered (F-319). Row 672 closed with that edit. Why: a NaN test in software would slow the comparison against the hardware's own; measured, it is noise on today's boxed compiled path and costs 15 to 20 percent a comparison on unboxed doubles in Java, and whether it stops vectorisation is open (`perf-probes/nan-check-cost/REPORT.md`). The team built `false` and `Unordered` everywhere and never raised the error, and Java, Swift, Python's floats, Julia and Haskell all answer `false`.
  from: compile-ladder/rung-spec-nan-comparison/REPORT.md, "1. What changed and why"; commit: a12ab5c60; note: a sentence that the tree made false, brought in line by the fewest words under the curator's rule of 2026-10-31 (explorations/coordinator/HELD.md, "Held in chat (2026-10-31)"): climb batch 26's 3eabf8435 revised both sentences of the overview of operators, and closed row 672 as FIXED; the review's finding F; what he decided, false and Unordered on both paths with no raise, is unchanged.

## The checker and the one library

- F-?1 **The compiled checker reads a helper's Java `String` parameter as the one library's `String`, a parameter that walk refuses at load, so the count and distance stages pass a binding that walk refuses.** Where it checks the one library, as the count and distance stages do, the checker reads a helper's Java types as the one library's (F-298), and its table maps a Java `String` to `String` (`compiler/NamingCzar.java`, `oneLibraryForeignJavaTranslations`). Walk refuses a helper parameter declared `String`, since a Java `String` cannot carry a string that is not flat (F-299). The two readings differ, and this bears on the design of the switch-over, where the compiled checker reads the one library's bindings. Read in the code. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-string-natives/REPORT.md`, "Decisions", item 2; `reviews/string-natives-2026-11-05.md`.
